data.content:
The lightest approach depends on your target framework:

### .NET 8+ → use the built-in `TimeProvider`

No new abstractions needed. Accept `TimeProvider` with a default:

```csharp
public class InvoiceCalculator
{
    private readonly TimeProvider _time;
    public InvoiceCalculator(TimeProvider? time = null)
        => _time = time ?? TimeProvider.System;

    public bool IsOverdue(DateTimeOffset dueDate)
        => _time.GetUtcNow() > dueDate;
}
```

In tests, use `Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing` (one NuGet package):

```csharp
var fake = new FakeTimeProvider(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
var calc = new InvoiceCalculator(fake);
Assert.False(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 6, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
```

### Older targets → thin `IClock` interface

```csharp
public interface IClock
{
    DateTimeOffset UtcNow { get; }
}

public class SystemClock : IClock
{
    public DateTimeOffset UtcNow => DateTimeOffset.UtcNow;
}
```

Same constructor pattern — default to `SystemClock`, pass a stub in tests.

---

**Key idea in both cases:** replace direct `DateTimeOffset.UtcNow` calls with an instance member, default to the real clock so production callers don't change, and inject a fake only in tests. No DI container needed — just constructor arguments with defaults.

